            <ArticleTitle>Supraorbital neuralgia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>302-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Supraorbital neuralgia is a rare disorder clinically characterized by the following triad: 1) forehead pain in the territory supplied by the supraorbital nerve, without side shift; 2) tenderness on either the supraorbital notch or traject of the nerve; and 3) absolute, but transitory relief of symptoms upon supraorbital nerve blockade. The pain presents with a chronic or intermittent pattern. In addition, there may be signs and symptoms of sensory dysfunction (hypoesthesia, paresthesia and allodynia), and typical &quot;neuralgic features&quot; (lightning pain and exteroceptive precipitating mechanisms). However, sensitive and neuralgic features are not constantly present and seem to be more frequent in the secondary, usually post-traumatic, forms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
